WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.960
VIC IGLESIAS: Hi, welcome
to the Anthos deep dive.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.810
This will be part 1
of a 2-part session.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:11.820
My name is Vic Iglesias.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.450
I'm a product
manager with Google,

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:15.142
and I have with me my colleague.

00:00:15.142 --> 00:00:16.350
JOHN MURRAY: I'm John Murray.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.690
I'm also a product
manager at Google.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.940
VIC IGLESIAS: Let's dive in.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.160
First thing you need
to know about Anthos

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.310
is that Anthos doesn't
just target one persona

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.670
within your organization.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.640
There are many personas
that can benefit

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.980
from onboarding onto Anthos
and its technologies,

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.590
including the infrastructure
operators, the service

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.473
operators, and the developers.

00:00:36.473 --> 00:00:37.890
And for those
folks, there's going

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.860
to be a lot of
different benefits.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.312
Could be that you're looking
for consistency in the way

00:00:42.312 --> 00:00:43.770
that you manage
your infrastructure

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:44.930
and applications.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.277
You might be looking to put
security guardrails in place

00:00:47.277 --> 00:00:48.860
to make sure that
your developers have

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.975
the autonomy they need.

00:00:50.975 --> 00:00:52.850
And we'll walk through
some of these benefits

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.550
as we go through the talk.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.300
When we're talking
about Anthos, we really

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.210
talk about these
five main components.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.430
And today, we're going to talk
about the bottom three here.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.560
So we'll talk about Anthos
GKE, Anthos Config Management,

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.110
and Cloud Logging
and Monitoring.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.080
So these are really targeted at
the infrastructure operators.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.850
And in part one,
we'll focus here.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.910
And you'll see service operators
and developers and how they can

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.950
benefit from Anthos in part 2.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.320
So first, let's talk
about Anthos GKE

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.260
and why customers
choose GKE in general.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.490
We'll break this
down into four parts.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.550
First, it's really
easy to start.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.220
And this is a lot of the
ways that people get started

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.940
with Kubernetes in general.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.810
You've got a really intuitive
console that you can use.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.930
You've got CLIs that provide
the functionality that you

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.120
need in order to get up
and running and scale

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.110
your services.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.870
And you can do this all in
one click, or in one command,

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.180
and really have a lot of the
ongoing management handled

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.150
by GKE's SREs.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:54.620
So with that
simplified management,

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.370
what do we really mean?

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.270
Well, you get in-built
monitoring for all

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:00.650
your Kubernetes clusters.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.560
Whether they live on-prem
or in other cloud providers,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.260
you can hook those
all in and have

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.850
all of the monitoring
and metrics

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.060
show up in a single
pane of glass interface

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.590
within the Google Cloud console.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:15.007
So this is super helpful for
wrangling all the clusters

00:02:15.007 --> 00:02:17.090
that you might have and
all the workloads that you

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.000
have across your organization.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.140
Next, we have features like
release channels, which

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.777
help you with the
ongoing maintenance

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.860
that you might have with
your Kubernetes clusters.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.570
For example, upgrading.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.150
You might not want to always
upgrade to the latest version,

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.280
or you might want to upgrade
to the latest version

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.230
because it has a feature
that you're looking for.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:37.930
And so what we have
in these channels

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.870
is the ability to pin
to a stream of releases.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.390
So for example, if you're
looking for always the latest

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.370
release, you can pin
to the rapid channel,

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.470
and that'll give you the
release of Kubernetes

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.870
that we've added most recently.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:53.590
Some customers want to have more
stable releases that we know

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.300
have worked well over time.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.600
And they can pin to the stable
channel, which will give them

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.683
a slower upgrade cadence.

00:03:00.683 --> 00:03:02.350
For folks that are
somewhere in between,

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.660
you can pick our regular
channel and get the updates

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:07.640
as they've been vetted by
folks in the rapid channel,

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:11.500
and then will finally pass
on into the stable channel.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.180
So with features
like that, really,

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.220
we're optimizing for
ease of management.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.200
But we also optimize for
different types of workloads,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:19.780
right?

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.020
So if you have machine
learning workloads, or really

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.530
intensive data programs that
you're running on your GKE

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:29.470
clusters, you might need
some hardware accelerators

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.450
in order to enable those to
run at the highest performance.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.000
So with that, we
have the ability

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.302
to attach GPUs, TPUs, and
local SSD to your GKE nodes,

00:03:39.302 --> 00:03:41.260
all through the declarative
Kubernetes settings

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.720
configuration in a
really simple way,

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.030
to optimize those
workloads for you.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:47.710
Additionally, we
want to make sure

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.200
that you can run both
your Linux and Windows

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.370
workloads in the same cluster.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.480
So with this, we have support
for Windows Server containers.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.730
And they will just
show up right side

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.770
by side in the same
interfaces that I've

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.170
shown before, with the same
monitoring and metrics,

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:03.500
all in the same cluster.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:06.620
So really useful for
those mixed workloads.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.290
As you're thinking about scaling
up the amount of workloads

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.935
that you have in your
Kubernetes environment,

00:04:11.935 --> 00:04:13.310
you're going to
want to make sure

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:14.870
that your clusters
scale really well

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:16.820
and that your workloads
scale really well.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.320
So one way we've
helped with scaling

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.030
your workloads is to implement
cloud native load balancing.

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:24.930
With cloud native
load balancing,

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.830
you can avoid a second hub
for your requests coming

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:30.680
from the outside world
through load balancers

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.510
and go directly to your pods.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.450
So now, not only is
there one less place

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.310
where you can have a
single point of failure

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.020
for your workloads,
but also you're

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:43.040
getting to your workloads with
the least latency possible.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.600
Additionally, we
also think about how

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:48.900
you might scale up or scale
out your workloads in GKE.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.700
So for example, you might
use vertical pod autoscaling.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.450
When you have a workload
that can't really

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.810
scale horizontally, but
needs to scale vertically,

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:59.600
and you don't really understand
how much CPU or memory

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.670
it might use, you
can use vertical pod

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.550
autoscaling in order
to watch that workload.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:07.125
And as it reaches the
limits of what it needs,

00:05:07.125 --> 00:05:08.500
if it's available
in the cluster,

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.170
they'll automatically get those
resources allocated to it so

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.210
that it can keep running
and won't crash or move

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:15.020
to another node.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:16.520
These are the kinds
of features that

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.673
make running workloads in
GKE as simple as possible.

00:05:20.673 --> 00:05:22.340
Additionally, if you
don't want to think

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.190
about what size your nodes
should be for your GKE

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:27.750
clusters, you can use the
node auto-provisioner,

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.420
which will just
provision the right size

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:33.380
nodes for the workloads that
you ask from your API server.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:36.320
And finally, another way we've
helped with scaling Kubernetes

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.810
is to make sure that clusters
scale to large number of nodes.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:41.640
And recently, we
announced that we

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.420
can scale up to 15,000 nodes
with a single GKE cluster.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.800
So all this is to say
that customers choose GKE

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.290
because we continue to
innovate on your behalf.

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.270
And this is something
that we are constantly

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:56.760
working on, and working
with our top customers

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.350
to make sure that
their needs are met

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.100
and that those can be
used in the simplest

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:03.750
possible way for other users.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.690
We understand, though,
that not everyone can

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:08.430
run on GCP for various reasons.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:11.230
So we want to make sure that
you can run Anthos and Anthos

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:14.570
GKE in any place where you
want to run Kubernetes cluster.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:18.260
So with that, we'll start
talking about Anthos GKE

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:19.580
on-prem.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:23.280
And really, when you think about
on-prem deployment options,

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.040
you might ask yourself,
why do customers need this?

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.168
Well, one example might be that
they have a database somewhere

00:06:29.168 --> 00:06:30.710
inside of their data
center, and they

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:33.050
want to have that workload
as close to the database as

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:35.810
possible, so if they want to
have that Kubernetes cluster

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:37.483
inside their data center.

00:06:37.483 --> 00:06:39.650
Another reason might be
that they have data locality

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.420
restrictions, and
they have to have

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.030
a cluster in a
particular place, and GCP

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:45.260
doesn't have a region there.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.330
So for these types
of use cases, we

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.070
have Anthos GKE
on-prem, which will

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:52.270
allow you to deploy Kubernetes
clusters inside your data

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:53.680
center.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.500
And we'll walk through a high
level architecture of how

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.830
Anthos GKE on-prem works.

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:02.530
So first, when you're
trying to get Anthos GKE

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:06.250
on-prem installed, you'll point
your laptop, or a developer

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:10.510
workstation, at a vSphere
API inside your data center.

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:12.910
That vSphere API will
then provision what

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:14.760
we call the Admin Workstation.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:19.000
And this is a Linux machine that
bundles all of the utilities

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.272
and binaries that we
need in order to deploy

00:07:21.272 --> 00:07:22.480
the rest of the architecture.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.600
So this is really kind of the
seed node for the rest of what

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:26.680
we'll talk about.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:30.250
Once you have that admin
workstation stood up,

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.900
you're going to deploy
the Admin Cluster.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.070
And the Admin Cluster
is the control plane

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:38.860
that allows you to create
many user clusters.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:40.360
And those user
clusters are the ones

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.370
that we're going to hand over
to the end users of Kubernetes.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.870
This Admin Cluster makes sure
that the right number of nodes

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.540
is provisioned, that the
right plugins are installed

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:52.300
into your clusters, that
the right functionality is

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.030
available to your end
users, and will make sure

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:57.140
that that stays up to date.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.410
So once you have the Admin
Cluster, that control

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:03.400
plane, up and running, you can
start to create user clusters.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:07.660
And that is done by creating
a Kubernetes resource

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.450
and applying that into
the Admin Cluster.

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:12.928
Once you do that, the
Admin Cluster will

00:08:12.928 --> 00:08:14.470
look at the
configuration that you've

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:17.200
asked for, as far as what that
cluster should have in it,

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.570
the size it should be, how
many nodes it should have,

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.540
and it will go ahead and
provision that user cluster

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:23.170
for you.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.480
And then you can get some
credentials out of there

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.770
and hand them back
to your users.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:31.240
This can create up
to 10 user clusters

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.570
per GKE on-prem installation.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:37.770
And each of those 10 user
clusters can have 100 nodes.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.789
So this really
allows you to scale

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:41.740
to a large number of
Kubernetes clusters

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.290
within each data
center footprint,

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.330
and then provide those
to various use cases

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:48.840
within your organization.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:53.840
Once you have those user
clusters and the control

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.940
plane up and
running, you'll also

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:57.410
want to include load balancing.

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.720
And load balancing gets
traffic into your workloads.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.200
So the types of load balancers
that we have available today

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.230
are one in-built load balancer,
which is what we recommend.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:08.900
This is based on some
Google technology

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.960
that we provide
L3 load balancing

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.520
on behalf of the clusters.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:16.610
You could also
bring an F5 Big IP

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:18.800
if that was how
you were doing load

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.600
balancing in your organization.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:23.450
Or you could bring your own
load balancer that was not

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.700
fully integrated
by us and configure

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:29.330
that to point at your user
clusters and your admin control

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:30.640
panel.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.890
A lot of flexibility there
to get the right setup

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:35.290
that you need.

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.990
On-prem is one of the places
where we think about clusters

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:42.340
being required, but we also
know that you have other clouds

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:44.860
that you've been committed to
and have workloads running in,

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:48.730
so we want to make sure that
you can run Anthos GKE on any

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:50.095
of the other cloud providers.

00:09:50.095 --> 00:09:54.430
The next thing we'll talk
about is Anthos GKE on AWS.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:58.120
There's a lot of benefits
to having Anthos GKE on AWS.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:09:59.890
One is you get all
the functionality

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:02.140
that I've talked
about in the earlier

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:05.110
part of the talk, where we're
managing clusters for you.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:07.420
We're integrating
monitoring to those pieces.

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:10.120
And really, it allows
you to integrate

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.820
that functionality into
your existing AWS footprint,

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:15.130
so super useful stuff.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:18.730
The pattern with
Anthos GKE on AWS

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:21.640
is that you'll have a
jump host, similar to how

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:27.160
we had the user laptop in the
Anthos GKE on-prem example.

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:31.150
And that jump host will have a
binary on it called Anthos GKE.

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:35.410
And that binary will talk to
the EC2 API and provision,

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:37.990
again, an Admin Cluster,
which will act as your control

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:41.470
plane for other clusters that
then you provide to your users.

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:44.060
So once you've provisioned
that Admin Cluster,

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:47.290
you're going to go ahead
and run commands against it

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:49.060
to provision your user clusters.

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:51.460
Again, the same pattern
that we had before.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:54.850
What we do get as a benefit
by running inside of AWS

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:57.610
is we have an in-built load
balancer in elastic load

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:59.050
balancing, ELB.

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:00.730
That comes out of the box.

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:03.880
So you don't have to configure
or run your own load balancer

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:05.720
in that case.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:09.110
As far as features that are
available in Athos GKE on AWS,

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:10.850
you have the ability
to have HA Control

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:15.650
Planes, auto-scaling, in-place
upgrades, and your storage

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:18.290
backed by AWS's EBS.

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:20.540
If you don't have an
existing VPC in one

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:23.780
and just kick the tires, you can
absolutely create your own VPC

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.540
through the Anthos
GKE on AWS installer,

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:30.020
or you can choose to install
into an existing VPC.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.330
And all of these clusters
that you bring up

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:35.270
are going to be well integrated
with Anthos Config Management,

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.730
Anthos Service
Management, and Cloud Run

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:40.780
on Anthos, which we'll talk
about in part 2 of this talk.

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.145
So now that we have
clusters all over the place,

00:11:46.145 --> 00:11:48.520
how are we going to manage
all the workloads across them?

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:50.145
How are we going to
make sure that they

00:11:50.145 --> 00:11:52.900
are as uniform as possible,
that we know what's running?

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:56.120
Well, we're going to have
some challenges there.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:58.940
And Anthos is willing
to help you here.

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:01.990
So we know that users have a
lot of different multi-cluster

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:03.040
use-cases.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.230
We talked about some
around data locality,

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:06.760
being near to databases.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:09.088
You also might want to have
highly available services.

00:12:09.088 --> 00:12:10.630
You might have
different environments

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:12.500
that have different clusters.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:15.790
And you might have, for
example, a low-latency service

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:18.160
that you want to
have with clusters

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:22.230
that are in different regions.

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:24.590
So the first thing that
you might want to do,

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:26.930
if you're already
a Kubernetes user,

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:29.000
is attach a cluster to
Anthos that you've already

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.340
provisioned somewhere
through some other mechanism.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:32.150
And we allow that.

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:34.275
So you can bring your
own clusters to Anthos,

00:12:34.275 --> 00:12:35.900
no matter how they
were provisioned, as

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:39.623
long as they're compliant
of Kubernetes clusters.

00:12:39.623 --> 00:12:41.040
So the way that
you do that is you

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:44.070
would register them through
the Google Cloud console,

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:45.570
or via the CLI.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:50.310
And that would attach them
to our Anthos control plane.

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:51.780
The way that that
actually works is

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:55.170
that we have a agent that
runs inside of your cluster,

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.330
and that calls out
of your cluster

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:00.720
and connects to our control
plane in a secure manner.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:02.340
It actually encrypts
that connection

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:05.580
that's egressing
your environment

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.150
and makes sure that it's secure.

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:09.400
And then with that
connection nailed up,

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:11.790
we can start to do
higher order things,

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:14.130
like provide load
bouncing across clusters,

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:16.680
or just show you your
workloads running

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:19.680
for those clusters inside
of the Google Cloud console.

00:13:22.323 --> 00:13:24.740
Another thing that you get out
of attaching these clusters

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:27.080
is you get to start
turning on and off features

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:29.165
that you might want
to have using Anthos.

00:13:29.165 --> 00:13:31.610
So for example, you might
want to turn on Anthos Config

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:35.150
Management, which we'll
talk about in the last half

00:13:35.150 --> 00:13:35.900
of this talk.

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:38.400
You might want to set up load
balancing across the clusters.

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:40.940
And you can do all of that
through the Anthos UI.

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:45.080
Once they're attached,
they're going

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:48.450
to show up just like any
other GKE on GCP cluster.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:50.100
So you'll have
those listed here.

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.320
You'll be able to see which
ones should be upgraded,

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.420
what the capacity of
those clusters is,

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:55.650
and whether they're healthy.

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:58.200
So super useful stuff.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:00.830
So we can talk about
multi-cluster ingress.

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:04.670
Multi-cluster ingress allows us
to have a single load balancer

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:07.550
IP that can point
to multiple clusters

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:09.690
within our Anthos deployment.

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:12.980
So now I can route people
based on the lowest latency

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.350
cluster that would reach them.

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:17.840
And if I have a
failover scenario,

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:19.820
I can route to an
additional cluster that

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:22.400
may be in a different region
or a different zone or maybe

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:23.780
in a data center.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:25.340
So with these
features, we're really

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:28.100
trying to make sure that
Anthos has the ability

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:32.630
to bring to you higher
complex use cases in a really

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:35.720
simple way that maps to how
we do things in the Kubernetes

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:36.792
world.

00:14:36.792 --> 00:14:38.750
And with that, we're
going to go ahead and take

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:40.820
a look at how these
things fit together

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:42.565
through the cloud console.

00:14:42.565 --> 00:14:43.940
And we're going
to look at how we

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:48.950
manage our clusters from
GKE on GCP, GKE on AWS,

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:50.060
and GKE on-prem.

00:14:53.235 --> 00:14:54.020
All right.

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:56.420
Since we now have
seen how Anthos

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:58.760
can get its clusters
provisioned and we've

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:00.992
seen some of the methodologies
for how that's done,

00:15:00.992 --> 00:15:02.450
let's take a look
at what we end up

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:04.820
with when we put all of
these things together.

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:07.617
And we'll explore this
