00:20:07.820 --> 00:20:09.010
So that's it.

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:10.053
We've got things running.

00:20:10.053 --> 00:20:11.470
And now we'll hand
it off to John,

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:13.470
who's going to take us
through a little bit more

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:17.530
in detail what ACM, Anthos
Config Management, can provide.

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:19.090
JOHN MURRAY: Thanks, Vic.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:22.720
I'm going to talk a little bit
about Anthos Config Management.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:24.280
Anthos Config
Management helps you

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:26.830
manage all of your configuration
and policies at scale

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:29.040
and includes three
different components.

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:32.350
There's Config Sync, for
managing configuration, Policy

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:35.050
Controller, for managing your
governance and compliance

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:41.500
policies, and Config Connector,
for bringing GCP into Anthos.

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:43.350
First, let's talk
about Config Sync

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:47.430
and how it helps you manage
configuration at scale.

00:20:47.430 --> 00:20:51.440
So when you've got a large and
diverse hybrid environment that

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:55.230
runs on Kubernetes, you have
a couple of scale problems.

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:56.840
One is scaling your team.

00:20:56.840 --> 00:20:59.300
You've got many people
on the operator team who

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:02.430
need to apply configuration
changes to that environment,

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:07.110
whether it's on GCP, on-prem,
or in other public clouds.

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:10.130
And as that team
grows, making changes

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:12.230
against that environment
by running commands

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:15.550
to keep control becomes harder
and harder to keep straight.

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:18.320
You don't know who's doing
what, and you don't necessarily

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:20.600
have a record of things
that have changed.

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:22.977
On the flip side,
all of those clusters

00:21:22.977 --> 00:21:24.560
and all of the
different teams sharing

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:28.045
them get bigger and more
diverse in multiple locations,

00:21:28.045 --> 00:21:29.420
and it makes it
harder and harder

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:34.140
to roll out changes that
affect the entire environment.

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:37.150
So the way we deal with that
in Anthos Config Management

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:39.400
is through a tool
called Config Sync that

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:42.640
puts all of your configuration
in a centralized policy

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:45.440
repository that's based on Git.

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:47.560
That means everybody
in the operating team

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:50.560
can collaborate together using
methods that they typically

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:54.160
use for application development,
pull requests, and code

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:55.333
reviews.

00:21:55.333 --> 00:21:57.250
And that helps your team
work better together.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:00.340
And then by connecting that
multiplatform environment back

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:03.490
to that central repository,
it helps your environment work

00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:06.850
better by having a single,
centralized source of truth

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:10.060
for all of your configuration.

00:22:10.060 --> 00:22:12.880
Config Management stores
that configuration and secure

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:16.740
repository and then rolls it out
to all your Kubernetes clusters

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:18.383
and to Anthos Service Mesh.

00:22:18.383 --> 00:22:20.050
And that means all
of your configuration

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:22.600
is auditable, that there's a
record of every change that's

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:25.750
made to the environment
stored in that Git repository.

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:28.810
It's revertible, in terms
of being able to roll back

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:32.380
to the last best, good state.

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:34.900
It's transactional in that
changes can be grouped together

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:36.330
and rolled out all at once.

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:37.720
And it's self-healing.

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:41.530
That single source of
truth is kept in sync

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:42.940
with all of your clusters.

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:45.760
And any local changes are
guarded against drift,

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:50.460
so that you can be assured
that it is applied everywhere.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:52.320
How that looks in a
production environment

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:53.710
might be something like this.

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:56.790
So if you have Google
Cloud platform with a GKE

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:59.850
cluster running on the left and
an on-prem data center that's

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:02.070
running GKE prem
on the right, you

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:04.050
can connect those through
Cloud Interconnect

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:07.035
and have a single
common private network.

00:23:07.035 --> 00:23:10.860
The policy repository that
holds all of your configuration

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:13.770
can live on-prem or
it can live in GCP.

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:16.030
In this case, it's
running on-prem.

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:19.080
And that policy
repository allows

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:25.200
you to define your configuration
using abstract namespaces

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:28.550
or inheriting configuration
across multiple teams,

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:31.990
so you can define policies
that apply globally.

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:35.430
In addition to that, you can
specify if configuration should

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:39.930
be rolled out to a single
cluster or a set of clusters

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:44.490
when you need to configure
things differently.

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:46.980
Now let's talk about
managing policies at scale

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:49.450
with Policy Controller.

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:53.200
Policy Controller is included
in Anthos Config Management,

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:56.410
and it is based on the Open
Policy Agent Gatekeeper

00:23:56.410 --> 00:23:58.420
project in Opensource.

00:23:58.420 --> 00:24:01.810
It allows you to manage
policy at multiple stages

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:05.710
of config deployment, and so
you can inspect configuration

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:07.330
before it gets applied.

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:11.050
You can enforce policies
at the API layer,

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:14.120
inspecting every call that
comes into your Kubernetes

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:15.170
environments.

00:24:15.170 --> 00:24:17.830
And then you can run audits
using the same policies

00:24:17.830 --> 00:24:20.530
to look at historical
changes, or simply

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:24.730
just to inspect things, without
necessarily enforcing them

00:24:24.730 --> 00:24:25.600
actively.

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:28.030
And that means that
any client that

00:24:28.030 --> 00:24:32.920
is connected to your clusters
is respecting the guardrails

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:35.190
that you have in place
that enact your security

00:24:35.190 --> 00:24:37.670
and compliance policies.

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:39.310
Those policies are
diverse, and that's

00:24:39.310 --> 00:24:42.750
why we support custom
policies in Policy Controller.

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:45.500
But Policy Controller also
comes with the default policy

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:46.000
library.

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:49.090
And that includes 27
constraint templates

00:24:49.090 --> 00:24:51.430
that are used for
common use cases

00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:53.980
that we see all the
time with our customers.

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:56.800
And these are policies that
are maintained by Google

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:59.030
and included as part
of the distribution.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:01.750
That might be something like
restricting a RoleBinding

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:06.190
to only your corporate domain,
or ensuring that mutual TLS is

00:25:06.190 --> 00:25:08.320
turned on so that all
of the traffic running

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:10.120
over your service
mesh is encrypted

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:11.590
in transit at all times.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:17.400
Finally, let's talk about
managing GCP resources

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:20.780
as part of Anthos.

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:25.070
GCP Config Connector
is designed to expose

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:30.200
all of the popular GCP services
that our customers use as part

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:33.440
of Kubernetes and Anthos.

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:38.450
It does that by taking
every popular GCP API

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:41.330
and turning it into
a Kubernetes API,

00:25:41.330 --> 00:25:43.090
so that when you're
deploying applications

00:25:43.090 --> 00:25:46.940
through kubectl, through
a git-based deployment

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:50.420
like Config Management
or through API clients,

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:53.030
you can treat GCP as
though it were just

00:25:53.030 --> 00:25:56.270
another part of that
Kubernetes environment.

00:25:56.270 --> 00:26:00.350
That means you've got a
native API service for GCP.

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:04.193
And you can specify
the resources

00:26:04.193 --> 00:26:06.360
that you would like that
underlie your applications,

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:08.900
whether that's a database
or storage bucket

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:11.090
in declarative configuration.

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:14.000
And that configuration will
be continuously reconciled

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:19.970
against the cloud and
guarded against drift.

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:23.390
This is available now,
and it is included as part

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:25.470
of Anthos Configuration.

00:26:25.470 --> 00:26:28.790
So all these components can
be installed separately,

00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:30.248
but they can all
be used together.

00:26:30.248 --> 00:26:32.540
And that means you can store
all of your configuration,

00:26:32.540 --> 00:26:35.980
whether it's Kubernetes,
Service Mesh, or GCP

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:38.750
in a single common
Git repository,

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:41.840
roll that out, and
check it for policies

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:46.610
that ensure that it's
compliant with your security

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:49.940
rules and your
operational controls,

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:54.470
and extend that way of managing
configuration from Kubernetes

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:56.120
all the way to GCP.

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:59.310
And that's Anthos
Config Management.

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:01.343
That concludes part
1 of our deep dive.

00:27:01.343 --> 00:27:02.760
Stay tuned for
part 2, where we'll

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:07.160
talk about Cloud Run for
Anthos and Anthos Service Mesh.

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:10.640
On behalf of Vic and myself
and all of Google Cloud,

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:12.910
thanks for joining us today.

